    "body": "Closes #13153\n\n### Description\n\nAdds a global `clipboard` configuration setting for OAuth device flows. Device codes are copied by default, and users can disable this persistently with:\n\n```shell\ngh config set clipboard disabled\n```\n\nBoth `gh auth login` and `gh auth refresh` respect the preference without requiring a flag each time. Explicit `--clipboard` and `--clipboard=false` values override the configured preference for a single invocation.\n\nThis also replaces the config option's `PerHostOnly` boolean with a `ConfigScope` enum so global-only, host-only, and global-or-host settings are represented directly. The new global-only `clipboard` setting rejects `--host` rather than writing an ineffective host-scoped value, while existing settings retain their previous scope behavior for compatibility.\n\n### How did you test this change?\n\nI built the changed `gh` binary and ran it with isolated configuration and credentials. I enabled `clipboard`, started `gh auth login` without passing `--clipboard`, saw the one-time code copied to the clipboard, cancelled before authenticating, and confirmed `pbpaste` returned the same code. Automated tests cover the enabled default, persistent disablement, and explicit flag precedence.\n\n![Terminal demonstration of the clipboard configuration](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/dfd1c585-2483-4f81-b1fa-65aafb01ad39)\n\n### Key points\n\n- `clipboard` defaults to `enabled`.\n- Explicit flags take precedence: `--clipboard` enables copying and `--clipboard=false` disables it.\n- Users can persistently opt out with `gh config set clipboard disabled`.\n- The preference applies consistently to both login and refresh device flows.\n- No config migration is required because existing files receive the application default.\n\n### Notes for reviewers\n\nStart with the configuration schema and scope model in `internal/config/config.go`, then review clipboard precedence in `pkg/cmd/auth/shared/login_flow.go` and its use by login and refresh.\n\n### Authorship and follow-up\n\nWho wrote this:\n\n- [ ] A human wrote it.\n- [x] An agent wrote it under close human direction.\n- [ ] An agent wrote it independently, and no human has guided the implementation beyond the initial prompt.\n\nWho answers review comments:\n\n- [x] @williammartin will read and reply directly.\n- [ ] An agent will draft replies and @username will read them before they are posted.\n- [ ] Nobody has explicitly committed to replying.",
